## This template

Runs off **USART2** (PA2/PA3 @ 115200), the **user button on PA0** (EXTI line 0),
and two LEDs. No sensors — the point is the concurrency layer.

### Pinout

| Function | Pin | Notes |
|----------|-----|-------|
| USART2 TX | PA2 | AF7, 115200 8N1 |
| USART2 RX | PA3 | AF7 |
| User button | PA0 | EXTI0, rising edge; board has an external pull-down (Discovery B1) |
| Press LED | PD12 | toggled by `Reporter` on each debounced press |
| Heartbeat LED | PD13 | toggled by `Heartbeat`'s own task at 1 Hz |

### What `main.cpp` does

Built on the SDK concurrency layer (`STM32_USE_SYSTEM` + FreeRTOS): `import
driver.stm32f4.exti; import system.executor; import system.timer; import
system.signal_bus;`. Three components share one `SingleThreadExecutor`:

- `Button<GpioPin>` — owns an `ExtiLine` and a debounce `Timer`. The
  `EXTI0_IRQHandler` calls `btn.irqHandler()`, which clears the pending bit and
  invokes `onIsr()` **in interrupt context**. `onIsr()` does nothing but
  `exec.postFromISR(edgeItem)` — the classic defer-out-of-ISR pattern. `onEdge()`
  (executor task) arms the one-shot debounce `Timer`; `onDebounced()` re-reads
  PA0 and, if still high, publishes a `ButtonEvent{seq, tick}`.
- `Reporter<Uart, GpioPin>` — a **reactive** component with **no task**. In
  `onBind()` it subscribes `onPress()` to the channel; each press toggles PD12
  and prints `press #<n> @tick=<t>`.
- `Heartbeat<Uart, GpioPin>` — owns a `rtos::Task` **created in `onStart()`**
  (deferred-start), not in `main`. Its `run()` toggles PD13 and prints
  `alive #<n>` every second.

The channel is `Channel<ButtonEvent, 4, 8>`: **ring depth 8**, so a burst of
presses that arrives before the executor drains is delivered in full instead of
coalescing to the latest (the default depth 1 keeps only the newest).

Flow:

1. `__initialize_hardware()` enables GPIOA, GPIOD and USART2 clocks (SYSCFG is
   enabled by the `ExtiLine` constructor).
2. `struct ButtonDemo` is the composition root: pins, UART, the executor, the
   channel, then the three components wired by reference. `ExtiLine` is the
   **last** member of `Button` so the deferred `WorkItem`/`Timer` exist before
   the NVIC line is unmasked.
3. `app.boot()` runs `system::bootstrap(btn, reporter, heartbeat)` —
   `Register → Init → Bind → Start`. `Reporter::onBind()` subscribes;
   `Heartbeat::onStart()` creates its task.
4. `main` prints the `BootReport` and starts the scheduler.

### Expected behaviour

- On boot USART2 prints `=== button-events-demo bootstrap ===` then
  `all components started; press the user button (PA0)`.
- `alive #<n>` appears once per second (PD13 blinks at ~0.5 Hz).
- Pressing the user button prints `press #<n> @tick=<t>` and toggles PD12; the
  debounce filter (20 ms) suppresses contact bounce, so one physical press ⇒ one
  line.

### How to verify

1. Power the board, open the serial port at 115200 8N1.
2. Confirm the bootstrap banner and the steady `alive #...` heartbeat.
3. Press the user button; confirm exactly one `press #...` line per press with a
   monotonically increasing `seq`, and PD12 toggling.

### When to extend

- Raise the debounce window or switch the EXTI trigger to `ExtiTrigger::Both` to
  report release as well as press.
- Add EXTI lines on other pins (lines 5–9 share `EXTI9_5_IRQn`, 10–15 share
  `EXTI15_10_IRQn`): call each line object's `irqHandler()` from the shared
  vector — every object checks its own pending bit.
- Give `Reporter` a sibling subscriber (`MaxSubs` is 4) that counts presses and
  publishes a derived event on another channel.
